# Test: IPv6 route reflection config acceptance
# VALIDATES: IPv6 RR client config parses with route-reflector-client + next-hop self
# VALIDATES: cmd-1 AC-13 -- peer detail shows route-reflector-client and next-hop
# STATUS: partial -- this file covers config parse and peer detail only; wire
# forwarding proof remains blocked by the single-ze-peer multi-IP timing issue
# PREVENTS: IPv6 RR config not parsed, next-hop self not wired for IPv6 peers
#
# NOTE: Wire-level forwarding verification (ORIGINATOR_ID in forwarded UPDATE)
# requires two peers on different IPs which is blocked by the single-ze-peer
# multi-IP timing issue. Unit tests verify the wire attribute modification.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=15s

# Send IPv6 UPDATE with MP_REACH_NLRI
# ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH=65001, LOCAL_PREF=200, MP_REACH: AFI=2 SAFI=1 NH=2001:db8::1 NLRI=2001:db8:1::/48
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF004B02000000344001010040020602010000FDE9400504000000C8900E001C0002011020010DB8000000000000000000000001003020010DB80001
# Assert ze's ipv6/unicast End-of-RIB.
#
# DO NOT "fix" this back to the ipv4 EOR (FFFF..00170200000000). It carried that
# hex until 2026-07-16, copy-pasted from the ipv4 siblings (nexthop-self.ci,
# rr-basic.ci) onto a session that negotiates ipv6/unicast ONLY (see the family
# stanza below). The ipv4 EOR was therefore unreachable by construction and this
# test had never asserted anything: the expectation was never evaluated, because a
# harness defect let expect=exit:code=0 disable the peer's verdict entirely
# (fixed in aaefef8ce).
#
# The hex below is DERIVED, not observed. RFC 4724 Section 2 specifies the
# End-of-RIB marker as an empty UPDATE for ipv4/unicast, but for every other
# AFI/SAFI as an UPDATE carrying only MP_UNREACH_NLRI. ze implements exactly that
# in message/eor.go:79-101: flags FlagOptional|FlagExtLength (0x80|0x10 = 0x90),
# code AttrMPUnreachNLRI (0x0F), extended 2-byte length 0x0003, value AFI=0x0002
# SAFI=0x01. Hence: marker | len 0x001E | type 0x02 | withdrawn 0x0000 |
# attrlen 0x0007 | 900F0003000201. ze was only afterwards confirmed to emit these
# bytes byte-for-byte. ze is correct here; the old expectation was the bug.
#
# Cross-checked against ExaBGP's independent EOR implementation, which packs
# ipv6/unicast to this exact hex and ipv4/unicast to
# FFFF..00170200000000, precisely the marker that was wrongly used here.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001E0200000007900F0003000201
EOF_PEER

# Native observer checks the initial-sync barrier and RR detail fields.

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external rr-ipv6-test {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/nexthop-self-ipv6-forward"
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer peer-src {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65000
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			route-reflector-client true
			next-hop self
			family {
				ipv6/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
